        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01MEF1qyeAboC8XSBh6D9Sep",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "     1→---\n     2→title: PostHog Setup - Edit\n     3→description: Implement PostHog event tracking in the identified files, following best practices and the example project\n     4→---\n     5→\n     6→For each event in .posthog-events.json, add PostHog capture calls to the exact file path specified. Do not relocate events to different files.\n     7→\n     8→Carefully examine the included example project code: your implementation should match it as closely as possible.\n     9→\n    10→## Client vs Server\n    11→\n    12→Events must be captured where they occur:\n    13→\n    14→- **Client-side files** (UI components, browser code): Use the client SDK to capture events\n    15→- **Server-side files** (API routes, server actions, backend handlers): Use the server SDK to capture events, if server-side code exists\n    16→\n    17→Do not move server events to client code. If an event was planned for a server file, implement it there using the server SDK. Create any necessary helper files for server-side PostHog initialization.\n    18→\n    19→## Correlating users across client and server\n    20→\n    21→Call `identify()` on the client during login/signup events. To correlate server-side events with the same user:\n    22→\n    23→1. Pass `X-POSTHOG-DISTINCT-ID` header from client to server\n    24→2. Use that distinct ID when capturing server-side events\n    25→\n    26→This ensures user behavior from both domains appears under the same user in PostHog.\n    27→\n    28→## Implementation guidelines\n    29→\n    30→- Use environment variables for PostHog keys—never hardcode them\n    31→- If a file has existing integration code for other services, place PostHog code alongside it without removing anything\n    32→- Add useful properties to each event\n    33→- Add error tracking where relevant\n    34→- Consult the documentation and example project for correct patterns\n    35→\n    36→Remember: Do not alter the fundamental architecture of existing files. Make your additions minimal and targeted.\n    37→\n    38→## Status\n    39→\n    40→Status to report in this phase:\n    41→\n    42→- Inserting PostHog capture code\n    43→- A status message for each file whose edits you are planning, including a high level summary of changes\n    44→- A status message for each file you have edited\n    45→\n    46→\n    47→---\n    48→\n    49→**Upon completion, continue with:** [basic-integration-1.2-revise.md](basic-integration-1.2-revise.md)\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
